/RC (<?xml version="1.0"?><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:11.0.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"><p dir="ltr"><span style="text-align:left;font-size:9pt;font-style:normal;font-weight:normal;color:#0000FF;font-family:Helvetica">Firmware is a term for a piece of software that is stored on a hardware device in order to make it run properly. It is not really meant to be i</span><span style="text-align:left;font-size:9pt;font-style:normal;font-weight:normal;color:#0000FF;font-family:Helvetica">nteracted with by the user once it is there. The firmware, once installed, is usually changed infrequently and only through updates from t</span><span style="text-align:left;font-size:9pt;font-style:normal;font-weight:normal;color:#0000FF;font-family:Helvetica">he manufacturer</span><span style="text-align:left;font-size:9pt;font-style:normal;font-weight:normal;color:#0000FF;font-family:Helvetica">.&#x20;</span></p></body>)
/Q 0
/T (Faisal Kaleem)
/Contents (Firmware is a term for a piece of software that is stored on a hardware device in order to make it run properly. It is not really meant to be interacted with by the user once it is there. The firmware, once installed, is usually changed infrequently and only through updates from the manufacturer. )
/Type /Annot
/Subtype /FreeText
/Rotate 0
/DA ( /Helvetica 9 Tf)
/CA 1.0
